# Komodo stack secrets (services VM) — headless admin, Core<->Periphery
# passkey, webhook, and the FerretDB/Postgres credential pair.
#
# Copy to .sops.env and encrypt with:
#   sops -e -i .sops.env
#
# Key-set contract: this file, the real .sops.env and the test fixture
# tests/fixtures/komodo.sops.env must all carry EXACTLY the same keys — the
# stack-env-drift lint fails on any difference.
#
# decrypt-sops-envs (host) writes these to /srv/komodo/.env. Core reads them
# via `env_file`; compose interpolates the DATABASE_* / PASSKEY values into the
# postgres, ferretdb and periphery services from that same .env.
#
# 🚨 Use hex-only values (the generate lines below). The DATABASE_PASSWORD is
# substituted into a `postgres://user:pass@host` URL AND the values are
# decrypt-and-source'd; a '$', ':', '@', '/' or '#' would corrupt one or both.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# First admin, created on a FRESH database only (KOMODO_INIT_ADMIN_*). With
# KOMODO_LOCAL_AUTH=true (set in compose) this is the password login. No
# browser wizard. Single token — a space would need quoting through the
# decrypt-and-source path.
# Password generate: openssl rand -hex 24
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
KOMODO_INIT_ADMIN_USERNAME=admin
KOMODO_INIT_ADMIN_PASSWORD=changeme_openssl_rand_hex_24

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shared secret gating Core -> Periphery. Core presents it (KOMODO_PASSKEY);
# Periphery accepts it (PERIPHERY_PASSKEYS, interpolated from this value in
# compose). The two MUST match or every deploy fails the handshake.
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
KOMODO_PASSKEY=changeme_openssl_rand_hex_32

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Verifies inbound git webhooks (Forgejo -> Core /listener) that trigger
# redeploys. Must equal the secret configured on the Forgejo webhook.
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
KOMODO_WEBHOOK_SECRET=changeme_openssl_rand_hex_32

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The DB password, shared three ways: postgres initialises the `komodo` role
# with it (POSTGRES_PASSWORD), ferretdb connects to postgres with it, and Core
# authenticates to ferretdb with it (KOMODO_DATABASE_PASSWORD). The USERNAME is
# NOT a secret — it is the fixed literal `komodo` in compose (fleet convention:
# POSTGRES_USER = the stack name, matched by backup-prepare.sh's
# `pg_dumpall -U komodo`). If this password ever differs across the three, Core
# cannot reach its database.
#
# 🚨 POSTGRES applies the password via initdb on FIRST start only. To rotate on
# an existing /mnt/fast/komodo/pgdata, change this key then:
#   docker exec -it komodo_db \
#     psql -U komodo -c "ALTER USER komodo PASSWORD '<new value>';"
# Password generate: openssl rand -hex 24
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
KOMODO_DATABASE_PASSWORD=changeme_openssl_rand_hex_24
